      "writtenOn": "2016-11-28T21:40:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Interesting - I\u0027ve never used freezegun this way. I\u0027ve always used it within it\u0027s context manager and incremented the clock manually using `tick` [0].\n\nAre there specific advantages to doing it this way? After reading the docs it doesn\u0027t look like `start` actually advances the clock, is just \"starts\" the time specified at `freeze_time`, right? \n\nThis might be a heavy handed test - but we could add the context manager here and start the clock when the password is still valid, authenticate, and assert a notification wasn\u0027t sent. Then we could advance the clock until the password has expired and then assert a notification was sent for the related PCI-DSS reason.\n\n[0] https://github.com/spulec/freezegun#manual-ticks",
      "revId": "faf5a02bd4ebc178fed71c67899f1c7678e9b9c3",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
